this iconic dish is all about the  details french toast might look super  simple to make but looks can be  deceiving french toast is crispy on the  outside that silky custard on the inside  it's just so indulgent and delicious  what's not to love so over the last  couple of weeks we've eaten a ton of  butter and bread and syrup to find out  what makes the best classic french toast  recipe  [Music]  french toast is only as good as its  bread so that's where we'll start there  are a ton of options out there and we  tested a bunch of them  for french toast we want to use a bread  that's both sturdy on the outside and  spongy and tender on the inside  something like a brioche challah or  white bread  we also tested milk bread hawaiian bread  and sourdough but found they really  altered the flavor profile of the dish  ultimately hollow was our favorite we  don't recommend buying a pre-sliced loaf  because we want our slices to be the  perfect thickness  if they're too thin they'll fall apart  with the weight of the custard but if  they're too thick it'll be difficult to  cook the custard all the way through and  nobody wants raw custard we think about  an inch to an inch and a half is the  perfect thickness now this next part  might seem counter-intuitive but we  don't want to use fresh bread we want it  to be at least a day old using fresh  bread will result in dense and soggy  toast but don't worry if you need french  toast right now and your bread isn't  ready just bake your slices at 200  degrees fahrenheit for 10 minutes to dry  them out and then you'll be ready to  make your custard the custard is really  where the magic happens when it comes to  french toast the perfect custard creates  a crispy exterior with a silky rich  interior if you use too much milk or a  lot of sugar in your custard the egg  won't cook and you'll get wet and soggy  toast so a good rule we follow is that  for about every quarter cup of dairy we  use one large egg for our recipe we'll  be using six large eggs it's important  to thoroughly whisk the eggs so you  don't end up with clumps of egg whites  in your custard that will ruin an  otherwise perfectly golden brown piece  of toast then we'll add our milk heavy  cream brown sugar and salt finally we're  going to add our seasoning seasoning  your custard is crucial now you can  definitely get fancy with it by adding  things like cardamom orange zest nutmeg  cloves ginger lemon zest or even  liqueurs but we found that less is more  when it comes to seasoning so we're just  adding a little cinnamon  and some vanilla paste you can totally  make this with vanilla extract but we  think it's worth the upgrade to use  vanilla bean paste it just packs a  bigger vanilla punch and it's still  cheaper than vanilla beans we'll whisk  all of our custard ingredients together  until it's well combined remember we  don't want any egg clumps  now that our custard's ready let's add  our bread we want to make sure the  custard completely soaks the bread but  we don't want to soak it for so long  that it begins to disintegrate  yeah it's just super gross so we'll soak  the bread until it begins to feel heavy  in our hands and completely saturated  throughout this could mean anywhere from  20 seconds to a minute on each side  now that our bread has taken a little  custard bath it's time to cook we tested  several methods your cast iron has to be  seasoned super well or your bread will  end up sticking to the pan the oven is a  great method for making big batches but  doesn't get the crispy edges we want in  a truly great french toast  so ultimately we love using a non-stick  electric griddle it distributes heat  evenly across its entire surface and  it's also big enough to cook large  batches of toast at a time but if you  don't have a griddle a non-stick pan  works just as well now to get a golden  brown crispy toast it's crucial to  preheat whatever pan you choose by  preheating the pan the custard will  begin to cook immediately if the pans  cold from the start the custard will  spread to form a foot something like  this on the bottom of the toast we  recommend preheating your pan before you  start making your custard once our pan  is hot enough we'll lightly grease it  with a pad of butter  or something a little more than a pat we  like our butter here at tasty once all  of that butter is melted we'll place the  soaked toast on the pan and let it cook  undisturbed until the bottom is golden  brown which takes about two minutes then  we'll flip and repeat on the other side  oh she's pretty just look at how crispy  and golden brown that is make sure to  keep an eye on your heat as you cook if  it's too high the sugar in the custard  will burn before the custard is cooked  through all the way in the middle once  your toast is looking beautiful we'll  remove it from the pan and here's a  little tip wipe your pan between batches  to make sure the butter doesn't continue  to cook and leave burnt patches on the  remaining toast now that we've whipped  up perfect golden brown crispy toast  it's time to serve you can really get  creative with the toppings for this use  whipped cream chocolate syrup  sprinkles bananas you can go wild but we  think that less is more we love to serve  ours with a pot of butter and some maple  syrup did you know that there's a  difference between pure maple syrup and  pancake syrup pancake syrup is actually  made with corn syrup and maple flavoring  it's definitely a more affordable and  shelf-stable alternative but if you can  splurge we love using warm pure maple  syrup drizzle that right over the top of  your toast and if you want to get fancy  with it finish with a little flaky sea  salt the sea salt really complements the  sweetness in the custard and syrup we  like to add a little pop of color so  we'll serve ours with a mix of berries  we're just using our favorites here and  we're done all that's left to do now is  enjoy your work of art with your friends  and your family on a wonderful brunch  afternoon or just indulge by yourself  either way if you loaf this recipe you  can find it and many more on our website  at tasty.com what's that thing that you  put on french toast sorry are you saying  syrup how do you say it bro it's syrup i  think people say it like me  you
 

